    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-10839",
      "knowledge_point_id": "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x$ at the point $(-2,10).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)=2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2)=2(-2)-3=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-7$ and the coordinates of our point $(-2,10),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(10) & =-7(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-10 & =-7(x+2) \\\\ y & =-7x-14+10 \\\\ y & =-7x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=6x+2",
              "option": "0"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=-7x-4",
              "option": "1"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=-7x+4",
              "option": "2"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=6x-2",
              "option": "3"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "y=-x-2",
              "option": "4"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
