      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,9).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+4x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}+4x)=10x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=10(1)+4=14\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=14$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(9) & =14(x-1) \\\\ y & =14x-14+9 \\\\ y & =14x-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
